QSM Direct: optional TCP 8006 gateway (experimental, single-node PVE 9)
===================================================================

Release: 0.4.0+stock4.12. This explicit standalone profile shares TCP 8006 between
normal PVE HTTPS and authenticated TURN/TCP. Signalling uses /qsm-direct/ on the
PVE browser origin. Video/audio and input/clipboard are encrypted by WebRTC.
TURN control messages use plain TCP, WITHOUT TLS. See Encryption below.
Direct UDP remains the normal profile where available; TCP retransmission can
add latency under loss. The gateway supports a node behind router/NAT with
only one TCP port forwarded. It requires a non-loopback node IPv4 relay address.

Requirements and package installation
------------------------------------
Read INSTALL-STOCK.txt / README.standalone first for platform, VM and LXC setup.
No patched Proxmox debs are needed on stock PVE. Download matching assets from:
https://github.com/rdmitry0911/qsm-rd/releases/tag/v0.4.0-qsm-direct-20261025-stock4.12
Verify SHA256SUMS-stock4.12. Install as root on the PVE HOST:

  apt install ./qsm-pve-direct_0.4.0+stock4.12_amd64.deb \
              ./qsm-pve-direct-standalone_0.4.0+stock4.12_all.deb \
              ./qsm-pve-direct-gateway_0.4.0+stock4.12_all.deb

If the LXC helper is already installed, include its matching stock4.10 deb in that
same transaction. For new LXC setup install the separate helper as described in
README.standalone; it embeds the guest payload. NEVER install qsm-console-guest
on the PVE host. Package installation does not activate a new gateway.

HAProxy, coturn and nftables binaries are needed. They are Suggested rather than
hard dependencies: generic services can create extra listeners or disturb an
existing operator-managed service. If NONE of these services is already
installed/configured, install without starting their generic units:

  systemctl mask --runtime haproxy.service coturn.service
  apt-get install --no-install-recommends haproxy coturn nftables
  systemctl unmask --runtime haproxy.service coturn.service
  systemctl disable haproxy.service coturn.service

If installation fails, resolve its reported dependency error and remove the
temporary runtime masks when finished. For existing installations preserve their
services/configs and install only missing binaries by your normal procedure.
QSM uses separate units, private configs and a dedicated qsm-turn user.

Activation and router/NAT setup
-------------------------------
During a maintenance window (pveproxy and QSM signalling restart):

  qsm-pve-direct-gateway enable --relay-ip <this-node-LAN-IPv4>

Use an IPv4 address actually assigned to the PVE node, for example 192.168.1.20,
not the router's public IP and not 127.0.0.1. Existing guests are not stopped.
Then hard-reload PVE (Ctrl+F5).

For a node at 192.168.1.20 behind a router, forward:

  public TCP 8006 -> 192.168.1.20 TCP 8006

Open https://your-public-hostname:8006/ and use the console normally. No separate
public UDP range or signalling port is required. An existing equivalent TCP
forward can be kept. If the outside port differs, e.g. 18006 -> 8006, open the
PVE page at https://your-public-hostname:18006/: the browser uses that actual
hostname/port for both HTTPS signalling and TURN/TCP. The frontend keeps the
node/custom certificate; it must match the hostname you use.

--relay-ip is the local bridge/relay address even behind NAT; do not substitute
the public IP. HTTPS, PVE API/WebSocket traffic and TURN/TCP share the listener
on that specified node IPv4 and on IPv6. Other node IPv4 addresses are not
automatically exposed. The router/firewall must pass raw TCP, not only HTTPS
requests through an application proxy. The gateway does not reconfigure your
router or host INPUT/FORWARD policy. Normal loopback and established/related
traffic must remain allowed. Relay-to-bridge UDP stays local to the node.

Check after activation:

  qsm-pve-direct-gateway status
  qsm-pve-direct-ui status
  systemctl is-active pveproxy qsm-pve-direct-terminal qsm-pve-direct-signal \
      qsm-pve-direct-gateway qsm-pve-direct-turn
  ss -lntp

Gateway/UI should report enabled/active and the listed services active. Public
TCP 8006 belongs to the gateway; the original pveproxy listens on 127.0.0.1:8006.
Backend HTTPS 18006, signalling 18007 and coturn PROXY 13479 bind to loopback
only (coturn also reserves its configured 13480 port during setup). Gateway
units/certificate timer are enabled for boot. For the actual NAT test connect
from outside the LAN; a LAN test alone does not test the router's forwarding.

Encryption
----------
- PVE UI, authentication and QSM signalling: HTTPS/TLS. The gateway terminates
  this TLS and uses certificate-verified TLS again to both local HTTPS backends.
- Video/audio: SRTP, with DTLS-SRTP key negotiation between browser and QSM.
- Input, cursor and clipboard data channels: SCTP over DTLS.
- Outer TURN connection: ordinary TCP, not TURN/TLS. TURN service messages,
  temporary allocation identifiers and relay addresses are visible on the wire.
  The encrypted WebRTC payload inside TURN remains protected between browser
  and QSM; a relay does not decrypt the desktop/audio/input contents.

Thus user session contents are encrypted, but this profile does NOT encrypt
all TURN control traffic or provide an all-TLS transport. Ten-minute TURN
credentials are bootstrapped via authenticated HTTPS; their opaque username
contains no PVE username or ticket. TURN/TLS would need a separately designed
and configured TLS relay arrangement. This package does not provide TURN/TLS
on the shared port. Port 8006 alone does not make every connection HTTPS.

Scope and access control
------------------------
Automatic setup accepts only a single-node installation with a stock PVE UI
and absent/comment-only /etc/default/pveproxy. Custom settings require manual
review, including LISTEN_IP, ALLOW_FROM, DENY_FROM, POLICY and real-IP settings.
It never silently bypasses existing source ACLs. Clusters, source-IP policies
and existing reverse proxies are outside automatic setup. The patched native
viewer is not covered. See README.standalone for staged migrations retaining
native VM backends; installing the gateway does not downgrade PVE packages or
convert running VMs. The bitrate fix applies to either installation profile.

Activation changes /etc/default/pveproxy so the original HTTPS daemon binds to
loopback and trusts the gateway's real-IP header. PVE also uses LISTEN_IP for
spiceproxy: external SPICE access changes after that service restarts. Existing
SPICE connections are not migrated. Use a separate access plan if SPICE is
required. The gateway does not modify stock Proxmox program files.

HAProxy passes client addresses to both HTTPS and coturn using PROXY v2, which
also avoids returning a loopback mapped address to Firefox. Coturn's PROXY
listener remains private. TURN credentials are issued only after PVE confirms
VM.Console for the requested guest. Coturn allows only the configured node IP;
a dedicated nftables OUTPUT table further restricts its UID's UDP to the exact
currently bound QSM ICE socket tuples. Fifteen-second leases refresh every five
seconds and are removed on normal close. Crashes expire leases; a failed refresh
closes the peer. Arbitrary local/network UDP services cannot be relay targets.
The set starts empty at boot. No global firewall flush or INPUT/FORWARD ACCEPT
rules are added. Generic coturn users/configs are unaffected.

The frontend reloads a changed PVE/ACME certificate within a minute. No PVE
passwords/tickets, SDP or TURN secrets are logged by the gateway tools.

Disable and rollback
--------------------
  qsm-pve-direct-gateway disable

This stops/disables QSM gateway/relay units and its certificate timer, deletes
only its OUTPUT table, restores previous pveproxy/signal config byte-for-byte,
reconciles the UI and restarts pveproxy. It restores the prior signalling
active/enabled state. Removing an active gateway package performs this rollback.
Keep /var/lib/qsm-pve-direct-gateway/before.json until disable succeeds: it is
the rollback record. Disable does not remove standalone, convert guest displays
or restore previously replaced native Proxmox packages. Direct-UDP standalone
needs its original HTTPS/UDP reachability again after gateway disable.

Stock4.2 input update
--------------------
Reload the PVE page with cache bypass and reopen consoles after upgrading.
Exact typing is the default: one atomic action per source press/repeat, with
no waiting for heartbeat before the first character. Delayed delivery cannot
create extra native repeats. Physical holds and Raw input are available for
games/shortcut editors; these use guest autorepeat and a 200 ms stale-hold
watchdog. Raw forces physical mode. Check Guest first + Exact typing for text
entry if an older browser preference selected Raw. No guest reboot or desktop
reprovisioning is needed. Both the bridge and worker must come from the same
core package; a cached older viewer retains its prior behavior.

Console Settings also provides Esc/Alt+Tab/Ctrl+Alt+Del/Super buttons.
Stock4.4 adds Capture keyboard (full screen) for Firefox/Safari and Chromium;
use the capture button instead of system F11. See the instructions below.

Mouse / trackpad input (stock4.3)
--------------------------------
Exact clicks is the default for ordinary mouse clicks, trackpad taps/clicks
and touchscreen taps. A short click is one complete action sent on release,
including position and modifiers. Moving 4 CSS pixels or holding for 250 ms
starts a physical hold/drag; its motion/release stay ordered until the bridge
acknowledges the release. A 50 ms snapshot renews held state, and 200 ms without
confirmation releases it without re-pressing from stale queued snapshots.
Physical button holds or Raw sends down immediately. Touch hold-to-drag stays
at 500 ms. Both scroll axes work and fractional trackpad deltas accumulate.
Normal double clicks pass; native double-click timing can still be affected
by delivery jitter. Generic virtual input cannot carry original click times.
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen consoles. No guest update is needed.

Fullscreen keyboard capture (stock4.4)
-------------------------------------
Reload PVE with cache bypass and reopen the console after updating.
Click the text Capture keyboard button near the top of the left toolbar
or the first action in Settings. Guest first alone is a routing policy.
The embedded console enters actual full screen for capture; its existing
page-maximize button still provides in-page enlargement separately.
Capture selects Guest first if Client first was saved, preserving Exact typing
and Exact clicks. Firefox desktop 151+ uses requestFullscreen keyboardLock:
"browser"; Safari uses the same API where supported. Chromium retains
navigator.keyboard.lock() and its permission flow. F11 is not required, so
macOS system assignments need no changes. Click the guest picture for input.

A short Esc is sent to the guest. Hold Esc or click Release keyboard to exit.
Switching to Client first or closing the console releases capture.
Opening Settings preserves fullscreen and releases guest-held input; close
Settings to focus the guest and resume capture. Stock4.5 pauses input on host app/tab switching without exiting
fullscreen, then resumes capture inside the existing fullscreen on return.
Held keys/buttons are released on blur; old holds are never restored. Guest first reserves Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Esc;
Raw keeps its existing no-QSM-chord policy. OS-reserved keys are not guaranteed
to reach any web app; use Send to guest buttons where necessary.

Headful Firefox 157 / Linux XTEST / real LXC checks cover ordinary Esc and
browser chords in separate and embedded consoles; complete production UI
smoke checks pass too. No physical macOS/Safari qualification is claimed.

Logical modifiers and host switching (stock4.6)
---------------------------------------------
Use Guest first to respect host modifier remapping (including a macOS
Option/Command swap). Printable positions still follow the guest layout.
Logical Command+Tab / Command+Shift+Tab stay on the Mac and are not sent to
the guest. Logical Option+Tab sends guest Alt+Tab. With swapped host keys,
physical Option now means Command (host switch), and physical Command now
means Option (guest switch). A browser cannot override native macOS app
switching or a third-party global shortcut; do not promise exclusive capture
of those bindings. The existing Alt+Tab Send to guest button stays available.

Outside Raw on macOS, Command is deferred until a guest action, avoiding a
stray guest Alt/Super/menu effect from host Command+Tab. Complete keyboard
and exact mouse actions include logical modifiers. Physical main-key holds,
physical dragging and modifier-wheel input flush deferred Command as needed.
A genuine solo Command tap still sends Super, while blur/host routing cancel
it. Late source presses while unfocused and foreign repeats are ignored.
Raw deliberately uses physical positions/edges and bypasses host-only routing;
use it only when literal physical input is required. Reload PVE without cache
and reopen consoles after upgrading. No guest update is required.

Settings and capture controls (stock4.7)
---------------------------------------
Opening/editing Settings releases guest-held input while preserving full
screen. Close at the top right refocuses the guest and resumes capture.
Capture keyboard is a text action near the top of the left toolbar and the
first Settings action, visible without scrolling. Reopening Settings resets
its scroll position. Guest first alone selects input routing; Capture keyboard
requests the browser's full-screen capture. The separate embedded in-page
maximize control remains available. The reveal handle sits beside a visible
toolbar, avoiding overlap with its controls at smaller iframe sizes.
Explicit Release keyboard, Client first, close and long Esc remain exits.
Reload the PVE page and reopen consoles after updating; no guest update.

Fixed controls and one-way capture action (stock4.8)
--------------------------------------------------
The controls handle stays at the left screen edge when toggled. The toolbar
has its own inset, preserving access without overlap or moving the handle
according to toolbar width. The handle's accessible state follows visibility.
Capture keyboard now only enables/resumes capture; repeated clicks keep
fullscreen, including from Settings and an existing ordinary fullscreen.
Release keyboard in Settings is a separate explicit action. Exit Full Screen
also exits native capture in embedded and separate consoles. Outside native
fullscreen, the embedded in-page maximize keeps its existing behavior.
The button label stays Capture keyboard; its pressed state marks the request.
Reload the PVE page and reopen consoles after updating; no guest update.

Deferred host-shortcut prefixes (stock4.9)
---------------------------------------
Stock4.9 extends deferred shortcut prefixes to Alt, Ctrl and Shift as well
as Command. A host can consume Tab before DOM; pending modifiers then
cancel on focus loss without a stray guest Alt/menu press. Exact typing
sends modifiers inside the complete guest action. Solo modifiers and
modifier-only shortcuts complete on focused release. Physical main-key
holds, physical dragging and wheel input flush pending modifiers before
the guest action; Raw retains immediate physical edges. No debounce is
added to an ordinary key press. Matching modern host capability is required.

Configurable host shortcuts (stock4.10)
-------------------------------------
Stock4.10 replaces fixed host/browser exceptions with editable Host
shortcuts in Settings > Keyboard shortcut priority. Each rule has an exact
logical modifier combination and a Host/browser, Release keyboard, Toggle
fullscreen or Show/hide controls action. Add, record, edit, remove or reset
rules; preferences apply immediately and persist in this browser. Guest first
and Client first have separate lists. Raw bypasses the list. Default host
rules reserve Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab, Meta+Tab and Shift+Meta+Tab; Ctrl+Alt+
Shift+Escape releases capture. A per-rule Send to guest bypass is available.
No additional Ctrl+Alt+Tab remapping is installed. Host rules prevent guest
forwarding; native OS/browser execution remains subject to its own policy.

Reserved shortcut prefixes (stock4.11)
--------------------------------------
Stock4.11 fixes a remaining host-shortcut prefix leak: the OS can consume
Tab and deliver Alt/Meta keyup before blur while the page still has focus.
Modifier-only prefixes of an active Host shortcuts rule now stay local on
release; they cannot turn a host switch into a solo guest Alt/Super/menu tap.
This also means a deliberate bare reserved modifier uses the explicit Alt,
Alt+Shift or Super Send to guest button. Unreserved modifier-only actions,
normal guest chords such as Alt+F, explicit per-rule sends and Raw remain
available. The active editable rule list controls which prefixes are local.

Exact modifier-only shortcut rules (stock4.12)
---------------------------------------------
Stock4.12 makes completed modifier-only combinations explicit shortcut
rules. All 11 combinations of two or more Ctrl/Alt/Shift/Meta modifiers
are listed as Host/browser by default in both profiles. Existing browser
preferences gain missing defaults once, preserving custom actions. Remove
a rule or select Guest to send that exact combination immediately on first
release; later releases do not add taps. Deleted defaults stay deleted on
reload. Alt+Shift, Ctrl+Shift and Win+Shift no longer remain blocked as
prefixes of longer host rules after their exact rule is removed. Recording,
editing and Send to guest support modifier-only combinations. Main-key
chords are matched separately. Raw and bare host-prefix protection remain.
